%I A397696 #33 Aug 19 2026 00:35:27
%S A397696 1,2,3,4,4,4,4,3,4,5,6,6,6,8,7,8,10,11,10,11,11,11,13,13,14,16,18,16,
%T A397696 15,16,18,19,19,21,22,22,22,22,22,23,23,24,24,26,27,28,28,27,27,27,28,
%U A397696 30,31,32,32,33,36,37,38,36,36,36,37,39,41,41,40,40,42,42,43,43,42,42,42
%N A397696 Number of adjacent differences less than 1/n in the sorted list with multiplicity of the fractional parts of sqrt(k) for n <= k <= 2n.
%C A397696 Let x_0(n) <= x_1(n) <= ... <= x_n(n) be the n+1 values frac(sqrt(k)) for n <= k <= 2*n arranged in nondecreasing order with multiplicities retained. Then a(n) is the number of indices j with 0 <= j < n such that x_{j+1}(n) - x_j(n) < 1/n.
%C A397696 Only adjacent differences in this nondecreasing list are counted. The circular difference 1 + x_0(n) - x_n(n) is not included. If [n, 2*n] contains more than one square then the repeated fractional parts 0 are retained and yield zero adjacent differences.
%C A397696 The threshold 1/n is the natural spacing scale for n+1 points in [0,1).
%C A397696 Radziwill and Technau study the empirical measure of the normalized adjacent differences mu_n = (1/n) * Sum_{j=0..n-1} delta(n*(x_{j+1}(n) - x_j(n))) and prove that it converges weakly to a limiting distribution with density P(u). By definition, a(n)/n = mu_n([0,1)).
%C A397696 Consequently, lim_{n->infinity} a(n)/n = Integral_{0..1} P(u) du.
%H A397696 Noam D. Elkies and Curtis T. McMullen, <a href="https://people.math.harvard.edu/~ctm/papers/home/text/papers/sqrts/sqrts.pdf">Gaps in sqrt(n) mod 1 and ergodic theory</a>, Duke Math. J. 123 (2004), no. 1, 95-139.
%H A397696 Maksym Radziwill and Niclas Technau, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.16493">Gap distribution of sqrt(n) mod 1 and the circle method</a>, arXiv:2403.16493 [math.NT], 2024-2025.
%F A397696 a(n) = Sum_{j=0..n-1} [x_{j+1}(n) - x_j(n) < 1/n] = #{j: 0 <= j < n and n*(x_{j+1}(n) - x_j(n)) < 1}, where x_0(n) <= x_1(n) <= ... <= x_n(n) are the nondecreasing rearrangement, with multiplicity, of frac(sqrt(k)) for n <= k <= 2*n.
%e A397696 For n = 2, the fractional parts of sqrt(k), 2 <= k <= 4, are sqrt(2) - 1, sqrt(3) - 1, 0. In increasing order they are 0, sqrt(2) - 1, sqrt(3) - 1. The two gaps are sqrt(2) - 1 and sqrt(3) - sqrt(2). Both are less than 1/2, so a(2) = 2.
%e A397696 For n = 3, the four ordered fractional parts coming from 3 <= k <= 6 have three successive gaps, all shorter than 1/3. Hence a(3) = 3.
%t A397696 ClearAll[a];
%t A397696 a[n_Integer?Positive] :=
%t A397696  Module[{x}, x = Sort[FractionalPart[Sqrt[Range[n, 2 n]]]];
%t A397696  Total[Boole[# < 1/n] & /@ Differences[x]]];
%t A397696 Table[a[n], {n, 1, 100}]
%K A397696 nonn
%O A397696 1,2
%A A397696 _Lark Song_, Aug 02 2026
